README.OSK -- GNU m4 0.50 for OS-9/68000, the source of CMDS/m4WHAT THIS IS  The OS-9/68000 port of GNU m4 0.50 by Stephan Paschedag (named in the  EFFO Info record: "from FREE SOFTWARE FOUNDATION (adaption to OS-9/68k  : Stephan Paschedag)"), exactly as it came: the archive m4_0.5.tar.Z  in the GNU directory of Microware's OS-9 archive.  The same tree is on  EFFO forum disk 14 as SOFTWARE/C/M4, file for file, with two more  files (info_m4, texinfo.tex).  Nothing in this tree has been changed;  it was already CR-terminated ASCII.  Two files of the archive are left out here and kept in ORIG:    m4       the built module -- it is CMDS/m4, byte for byte    m4.dvi   TeX output of m4.texinfo, which is hereHOW IT RELATES TO THE SHIPPED BINARY  CMDS/m4 is the `m4' inside this very archive (and inside forum disk  14's SOFTWARE/C/M4, and m4_0.5.Z beside it): md5  d4de41609e04443c319b74e494b883de, 39086 bytes.  This is its source,  published with it by its porter.  SRC/eff_m4b is NOT GNU m4 at all.  It is PD m4 by Ozan Yigit ("PD m4  is based on the macro tool distributed with the software tools"),  from EFFO forum disk 9, PROGRAMME/C/M4/SYSSOURCE -- every .c and .h  there matches that directory exactly.  DOC/ORIGINS names forum disk  14 for it, which is where GNU m4 is.  The porter's OS-9 changes are the #ifdef OSK blocks and oskvprintf.c  (a _doprnt for OS-9).  version.h says 0.50.BUILDING IT  The porter's makefile compiles into RELS, renames the static `isp'  in input.c with -Disp=ibsp, and links os9lib.l.  With this  collection's driver, tools/rebuild, the recipe is    m4|m4_0.5|m4.c input.c output.c symtab.c builtin.c macro.c eval.c oskvprintf.c obstack.c|CPP2 isp=ibsp|/dd/LIB/os9lib.l|  CPP2 because Microware's cpp aborts (bus error) on input.c.  isp=ibsp is the makefile's own define, applied to every file, which  is harmless: only input.c names isp.  vfprintf.c is not compiled;  the makefile leaves it out too.  That build is NOT byte-identical to CMDS/m4 (it is -qm, trap-free,  52196 bytes).  Measured on 2026-09-24 against CMDS/m4, on all 39  EXAMPLES and 13 TEST files: standard output identical on every one  and standard error identical on every one; the only differences seen  were (1) the ORDER of the two when both go to one file, which is  buffering, and (2) the number sysval gives after a syscmd that  could not run -- both builds fail it (no `shell' module to fork) and  report a different nonzero value.  The archive binary still  ships; this tree is here so its source does.TERMS  m4.c, line 3: "This program is free software; you can redistribute  it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License  as published by the Free Software Foundation; either version 1, or  (at your option) any later version."  Copying is the GNU General  Public License version 1 in full.  vfprintf.c is Berkeley's, under  its own notice at the head of the file (not compiled).FILES  *.c *.h            the program; obstack.[ch] is GNU's  makefile           the porter's OS-9 makefile  ReadMe Plans       GNU's own  m4.doc m4.texinfo  the manual, as text and as Texinfo  EXAMPLES TEST      the manual's examples and GNU's test macros  dotest.zsh         the porter's test driver, for the OS-9 zsh  newvers.sh         GNU's; not used on OS-9  RELS               empty; the makefile's object directory